# Which commit the newest prebuilt blob was built from, and which later
# firmware commits have been VERIFIED not to change the compiled image.
#
# Why this file exists.  The `prebuilt freshness` CI job used to compare commit
# DATES: it failed when any commit touching src/, hal/ or ti/ was newer than the
# prebuilt.  That is right about the property it protects -- a stale blob shipped
# for 32 commits under a green `prebuilt integrity` (#75) -- but it cannot tell a
# real firmware change from one that provably does not alter the image.  #79 was
# the second kind: it guarded a global the linker was already dead-stripping, the
# rebuilt .out was byte-identical, and the gate went red anyway and STAYED red on
# every later PR.  A permanently red gate is how a real staleness gets waved
# through, which is the exact failure #75 was about.
#
# So the check is now an ATTESTATION, not a date heuristic.  Every commit
# touching src/, hal/ or ti/ after `built-from` must be listed under `inert:`,
# with evidence, or the gate fails.
#
# WHAT AN `inert:` LINE CLAIMS, precisely.  NOT that the shipped blob still
# corresponds to source -- for a WRAPPED release that is impossible on any
# permitted host, full stop: the last build stage signs with the vendor
# VALIDATION private key, which is deliberately never on a build host
# (keys/README.md: "The private half is not in this repository and never will
# be"), so 70 bytes of ECDSA-P256 signature TLV plus the 32-byte SHA-256 TLV
# over it are unreachable by construction (#94, #92).  An `inert:` line claims
# the narrower, checkable thing instead: THIS COMMIT DOES NOT CHANGE THE
# COMPILED IMAGE, established by rebuilding `raw-sha256` -- the stage-1
# build_ti.ps1 output, before flash-images-builder wraps and signs it (see
# BUILD_RECIPE.md) -- with and without the commit's diff, and comparing.  That
# raw half is the only one a source commit can affect; the wrap and signature
# add no source-derived content.
#
# CI cannot verify an `inert:` claim itself -- rebuilding needs license-gated TI
# ticlang, which is not in CI -- so the claim is only as good as the evidence
# quoted beside it.  Rebuild and compare before adding a line; do not add one to
# get a PR green.
#
# When cutting a real release: bump `built-from` to the release commit, update
# `image-sha256` and `raw-sha256`, and DELETE every `inert:` line (they
# described the old blob).
#
# This repo SQUASH-MERGES.  The commit that built the shipped blob almost always
# lived on a feature branch first, under a different SHA than the one that lands
# on main -- the squash rewrites it.  Once that PR merges and the branch is
# deleted, the pre-squash SHA is reachable from nobody's remote and a fresh CI
# clone cannot resolve it, even though the release commit it became is sitting
# right there on main.  This happened for real: `built-from` here pointed at
# 9eb7751 (a feature-branch commit from "release: cut prebuilt v0.6.0 for the
# protocol-7 seq change"), which squash-merged into main as 3d963c9 ("fix
# (sockets): cache SOCK_SEND's reply by seq..." (#89)).  CI failed every PR with
# "built-from (9eb7751...) is not a commit in this repository" until this line
# was repointed at 3d963c9 -- same blob, same sha256, just the SHA main actually
# has.  The same rot applies to every `inert:` SHA below, for the same reason --
# it is copied from a PR branch too.  `.github/scripts/check_prebuilt_freshness.py`
# checks both `built-from` and every `inert:` SHA for exactly this before doing
# anything else, and fails with this incident named rather than a bare "not a
# commit" -- only record a SHA reachable from `main`; a branch-tip SHA is a
# ticking failure the moment its PR merges.

built-from:   615c7236a0d5399263bf4c1a6d6a619f8a125fec
image-sha256: d58d78a6697ea6d74c12ee51912dfb0434214f4244add2e6af03928919b0eb9f
raw-sha256:   7db034a748840f2387725ef1388d5092c78fd6de517958938819f6130025772a

# The GPE anti-rollback floor the NEWEST shipped artifact must clear.
#
# The CC35 SBL enforces GPE-version monotonicity per part, against that part's
# last-seen version, even when every *_rollback_protection_* fuse reads 0.  A
# stamp below what a unit has already seen streams clean -- exit 0, the full
# ~1.09 MB -- and then the SBL refuses to boot it: dead link, empty XDS110
# `query` table.  It is permanent for that part.
#
# So an artifact stamped below our own bench units is unshippable, and no
# amount of documentation fixes it: re-wrapping at a legal stamp needs the
# signing assets, which a customer does not have.  This floor records the
# highest stamp any unit here has been flashed at, and CI asserts the newest
# wrapped blob clears it.  Raise it whenever a bench run flashes higher.
#
# History: releases 0.4.0/0.4.1/0.5.0 stamped SemVer-shaped values (0.4.0.0
# etc.) and an earlier cut of 0.5.1 did the same.  That convention is broken by
# construction -- every bench or OTA-iterated unit climbs into 0.149.x through
# ti/regen_flashset.sh and can then never accept a 0.5.x.0 artifact again.
gpe-floor:    0.149.73.0

